This is a modified version of the RustDesk client, Copyright (C) Purslane Ltd.
and the RustDesk contributors, distributed under the same licence. This file
exists to satisfy the licence and is the only place the prior work is named;
the product, its binaries and its documentation are nremote. Dependency source
URLs still resolve from that project's repositories, because that is where
those crates live.

Modified by NDDev beginning 2026-09-02. Changes so far:

  * The shared protocol library under libs/hbb_common, previously consumed as
    a git submodule, is vendored so this repository owns every line it builds.

  * Every outbound call the client made on its own behalf is removed. The daily
    version check reported the host's distribution, OS version, architecture
    and a device fingerprint to a third-party endpoint; it is gone, along with
    fingerprint.rs, the module that computed the fingerprint. The auto-updater
    that downloaded and installed releases from that endpoint is disabled at
    its entry points, because the request was the only thing that ever set the
    URL it downloaded from. The remaining unreachable platform installer and
    scheduler code was subsequently deleted; only an explicitly requested
    release-asset download path remains. The default API server no longer falls
    back to the vendor's hosted console.

  * A third party's signing public key is removed from the custom-client
    configuration path. It was a trust anchor: a configuration blob signed by
    whoever holds the private half would have been accepted and applied.

  * The compiled-in default rendezvous server and its public key are cleared.
    Both are build inputs now, validated by scripts/configure.py, and absent
    from this repository because it is public and a deployment address is a
    fact about a private estate.

  * The product identity is renamed to nremote by scripts/brand.py, which is
    idempotent and whose --check mode is a CI gate.

  * The wire protocol shared with nremote-server is pinned by digest and
    checked on both sides by scripts/check_protocol_parity.py.

The rendezvous protocol is unchanged. This client interoperates with servers
built from the prior work, and nremote-server interoperates with clients built
from it, which is what makes an incremental migration possible.
